WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.043
- Say hello to Diego Calva everybody.

00:00:04.385 --> 00:00:05.218
- Hello.

00:00:05.218 --> 00:00:06.090
Hi.

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:07.860
- [Kelly] Oh my gosh,
Diego, I'm gonna lead

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.060
with you're so talented.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:09.900
I love this film so much.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.370
You did such an amazing job.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:13.283
And also happy New Year.

00:00:15.117 --> 00:00:15.950
- Thank you.

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:16.890
Happy New Year everyone.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:18.630
- How, how did you celebrate the New Year?

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:19.560
What did you do?

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.200
- Well, I like to spend it
with my mother and my family.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.344
And in Mexico we have this tradition,

00:00:24.344 --> 00:00:28.909
we eat 12 grapes and you have to-

00:00:28.909 --> 00:00:29.742
- [Kelly] Oh, I've done that.

00:00:29.742 --> 00:00:31.290
- to do a wish for every grape.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:33.510
But last year was so incredible

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.032
for me that honestly, I was wishes-less.

00:00:36.032 --> 00:00:36.865
- [Kelly] Aw.

00:00:36.865 --> 00:00:37.950
- Everything came true.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:38.783
- [Kelly] That's a beautiful thing.

00:00:38.783 --> 00:00:39.750
I've actually done that once.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:40.583
You just reminded me.

00:00:40.583 --> 00:00:41.550
I was in Mexico once for New Year's,

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.200
and they, they passed out the grapes

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.360
at the restaurant we were
at and I was like, what?

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.040
And I just like grapes
and I like wishes, too.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.420
So I kept going.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:49.530
I was like, I'm into this.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:50.520
- How, how many am I allowed?

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:51.725
- [Kelly] I know.

00:00:51.725 --> 00:00:52.740
I'm like, can I have more?

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.360
I wish for more wishes.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:55.710
Well, Diego, speaking of family

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:59.190
we have them to thank for
introducing you to movies, right?

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:00.023
- Yeah.

00:01:00.023 --> 00:01:02.957
First of all, Margot,
you look great as always.

00:01:02.957 --> 00:01:03.939
As always.

00:01:03.939 --> 00:01:05.522
- So do you, honey.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:11.550
- Yeah.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.350
My grandfather and my grandmother

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.000
they used to take me to the movies.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.930
Like, that was my, my, my
activity during the weekends.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.000
And it was like a very
special moment, you know,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.400
for a kid like going to the
theater and, and all that.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.761
- Yeah.

00:01:24.761 --> 00:01:26.190
- [Diego] And I remember before I was able

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:29.283
to read like the subtitles super fast.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.320
They love me so more,

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.200
that they whisper the whole
movie in my ear, you know,

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.970
they read the, the subtitles and whisper

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:37.770
like Batmam just said this,

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:39.930
or Superman just said that.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:41.240
Things like that.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:42.073
- [Kelly] Aw.

00:01:42.073 --> 00:01:44.843
That's a lot of love, cause
that's a lot of translating.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:50.010
But your character in Babylon
has a similar love of movies

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.200
so I love and know your character

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:53.033
but everybody else doesn't.

00:01:53.033 --> 00:01:54.870
So you wanna tell him a bit about Manny?

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.660
- Well, Manny, first of all,
he's still my best friend.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:58.500
I think I'm going to be

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.663
with that guy for the rest of my life.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.290
But then Manny is a dreamer.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.870
Manny is a guy that he
really wants to be part

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.540
of something bigger than what he knows.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:11.130
He, he, and for him

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:14.430
that something is movies and
he's going to do anything.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.680
You know, he, he doesn't
care if he has to clean

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:20.430
if he has to, to bring the
coffee, to cook like anything.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.890
He just want to be part
of that beautiful party.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:24.750
He's been seeing from the distance

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:27.181
that is like Hollywood and movie making.

00:02:27.181 --> 00:02:28.470
- [Kelly] Like I feel
like I was telling Margot

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:30.240
in the commercial break, I was like, it's

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.670
I feel like Manny is how everyone
starts when you come here.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:35.043
You're just such a dreamer
and you're just wide-eyed

00:02:35.043 --> 00:02:37.920
and, and it's a beautiful
thing that, that innocence

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.350
like coming to Hollywood or you know, LA,

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:44.190
but so Diego what was it like
when you first met Brad Pitt?

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:45.023
What was that like?

00:02:45.023 --> 00:02:46.260
Because in the, in the movie

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:48.990
it's kind of funny cause
he plays this big huge star

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:51.390
which not a stretch and
you know, and this is

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:52.920
like a big huge film
for you to do with him.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:54.720
So what was that like, like
having the scene with him

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.430
like working with him at first?

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.843
- Well, I wanted to
tell the story of how I

00:02:58.843 --> 00:03:02.100
I met Brad because with Damien everything

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.410
is so meta-fictional and Damien took care

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:06.120
at least with my character and with me

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.510
like to try like weird situations.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.460
So I was in Mexico City
and Damien called me

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.493
and he said, Diego, I'm going
to direct this commercial.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:16.890
Brad is the lead of the commercial.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:18.390
I think it's a good idea if you come

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:21.870
to, to Los Angeles and meet him.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:23.250
Also, there's going to be a bunch

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:25.200
of people that is going
to work in the commercial

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.820
that are part of the crew of the movie.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.400
So I think it's a win-win
kind of situation.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.260
So I flew to Los Angeles

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.960
and right before we started
like shooting the commercial

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.540
Damien told me he wanted me to pretend

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:40.320
that I was his assistant
or a PA on the commercial.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:44.250
So I worked for one day, I
had like my backpack full

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:46.170
of Coca-Colas and I was literally giving

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:47.790
like Coca-Colas to everyone

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:49.890
and, 'Hey Mr. Brad
Pitt, you need something

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:51.510
like a coffee or anything?

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.217
I can, I can help you.'

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:56.760
And day two of the shooting,

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:59.070
Mary Zophres, the costume designer,

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:00.480
she, she did Babylon with us,

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.880
also, she was doing the commercial.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:04.110
She came to Brad and said like,

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:05.760
'Hey Brad have you met Diego?

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.857
He's going to play Manuel in Babylon.'

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:11.430
And Brad just turned to see Damien

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.110
and to see me and he said, like,

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:14.670
somebody had something to explain.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:15.820
Damien what's going on?

00:04:17.411 --> 00:04:19.290
And I, he's a very, a very funny way

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:20.610
to meet Brad Pit, actually.

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:23.310
I think the shock was in his
side, not in mine, this case.

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:24.570
- [Kelly] I know, I love that.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:25.560
He was like, wait, what?

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.060
The guy with the Coca-Cola, right?

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:28.590
He was like, wait, what?

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:31.440
So Diego, you also stayed, this I love,

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.030
you, actually stayed with Margo

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:34.470
and her husband while filming.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:35.303
Right?

00:04:35.303 --> 00:04:36.301
- We're roomies.

00:04:36.301 --> 00:04:37.581
- [Kelly] I love that.

00:04:37.581 --> 00:04:38.414
- [Diego] Yep.

00:04:38.414 --> 00:04:39.247
- [Kelly] What was that like?

00:04:39.247 --> 00:04:40.631
- [Diego] I said this before,

00:04:40.631 --> 00:04:42.780
but I still very grateful
and I really appreciate

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:46.020
that because sometimes Hollywood
could be a lonely place.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:46.853
You know-

00:04:46.853 --> 00:04:47.919
- [Kelly] A lot of times.

00:04:47.919 --> 00:04:48.752
- [Diego] Especially when you're

00:04:48.752 --> 00:04:49.585
like the newcomer or like the new kid

00:04:49.585 --> 00:04:52.050
and you don't have like your
friends and your family there.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:52.973
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:52.973 --> 00:04:54.540
- So I was working on Babylon all day

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.130
and then going back to the hotel

00:04:56.130 --> 00:04:57.990
and sleeping by myself, like, you know

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:00.810
it could, could be like a
tricky, lonely situation.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:03.360
So I think Mar Margo saw that on me

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.760
and she just invited me
and it was incredible

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:07.440
because for a couple of weeks you feel

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.030
like, like home again

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:11.190
and you are not like lonely
and just like sharing,

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:14.250
you know, like playing cards
or, or playing with Belle.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.110
By the way, Belle is the
most beautiful people

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:17.997
you will ever, ever see.

00:05:17.997 --> 00:05:18.830
- [Margot] That's my dog.

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:19.663
- That's your dog.

00:05:19.663 --> 00:05:20.496
I was like, is that the pit bull?

00:05:20.496 --> 00:05:21.368
- Yeah.

00:05:21.368 --> 00:05:22.541
She's getting so much airtime today.

00:05:22.541 --> 00:05:23.374
- [Diego] That situation is very helpful.

00:05:23.374 --> 00:05:25.350
- [Margot] No, he's
Diego's the best roommate

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:27.480
because yeah my dog loves him.

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:30.390
So it, we were like
co-parenting my pit bull

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:32.010
and we were on the same hours

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:33.690
we're doing all these crazy hours at work.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:37.170
And so we were, you know, it's
actually the juxtaposition

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:39.840
between our day life and our nightlife.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.420
It's like a sign of, you know

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:44.490
a show of domesticity when we get home

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:46.680
and at work it's like
crazy having fight scenes

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:48.330
and doing these scenes
where we're doing tons

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:50.430
of drugs and partying and
then we'd get home and be like

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:51.270
have you fed Belle?

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:52.289
I'll take her for a walk.

00:05:52.289 --> 00:05:54.390
Do we, do we need more milk?

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:55.590
I'll get milk tomorrow.

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:56.460
Okay, great.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.620
And then we go, go back
to work and it's like rah.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:00.120
It's great.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:01.101
- I, I love it.

00:06:01.101 --> 00:06:02.460
I just think it's a cool
thing too, just to like

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.080
you know for the couple
weeks or whatever it was

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.510
just to be able to like,
you know, hang with people

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:08.670
outside of work that you're,
you have this chemistry

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:10.500
on set and you're building
something beautiful.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:11.333
- Yeah.

00:06:11.333 --> 00:06:12.166
- [Kelly] That's long lasting.

00:06:12.166 --> 00:06:13.050
And that's a, that's a really cool thing.

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:16.000
I thought that was like my
favorite part of this interview.

